# Titan Orbit — FPS / game-speed warning

Evaluate **every** request that would change **runtime** client or server work. Judge two costs:

| Cost | Feels like | Typical cause |
|------|------------|---------------|
| **FPS** | Client hitch, stutter, dropped frames | Render work, `GC.Alloc`, main-thread loops |
| **Game speed** | Whole match slows / ticks hitch | Server sim overrun, fat ghosts, extra RPCs, prediction cost |

Sim is a fixed ~60 Hz NetCode step. A server hitch is not “prettier visuals” — it is the game running late.

---

## When to warn

If the request implies any of these, **lead with the warning block**, then implement a **cheaper alternative**. Do not wait for the user to notice.

- **Tick / hot-path allocations** — `new`, LINQ, strings, boxing, or `GC.Alloc` in ECS sim, prediction, or `Update`/`LateUpdate`. HUD already throttles this (`ShipSpeedometerHUD`).
- **Main-thread / managed loops over many bodies** — per-frame `EntityManager` gathers, `Find`/`GetComponent`, or nested ship×planet scans instead of a cached query + Burst `IJobEntity`.
- **Extra GameObjects / colliders / visual copies** — wrap tiles, per-body proxies, or Instantiates that fight `EcsWorldVisualizer` per-frame spawn caps and the toroidal “one entity, one collider” rule.
- **Fat ghosts / extra RPCs / extra sim work** — new serialized fields, per-tick broadcasts, or work that makes the ~60 Hz step miss.
- **Render-rate sim or double presentation** — driving motors from MonoBehaviour `Update`, extra LateUpdate lerps on top of NetCode, or bypassing `ClientJoinSettleCache.ShouldSkipMapBodyQueries`.

**Prefer:** Burst jobs, cached queries, pooling, presentation-only work at a capped rate, lean ghost fields, existing visualizer/HUD budgets.

If the user **insists** on the expensive path after the warning, do it and keep the cost note.

---

## When to stay silent

Do **not** add a warning footer on every reply. Silence is correct for:

- Docs, comments, Cursor rules, `.mdc`, editor-only / `#if UNITY_EDITOR`
- One-shot load, bake, or init (not per tick / per frame)
- Reusing an existing cheap path (cached query, pool, Burst job, visualizer cap)

---

## Response shape (only when warning)

Put this **first**, then continue with the cheaper plan:

```markdown
## FPS / game speed
**Risk:** High | Medium
**Why:** One sentence (what would hitch: render, GC, or server tick).
**Cheaper path:** What I will do instead. Say to insist if you still want the expensive version.
```

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — alloc + managed gather every tick (GC stutter + main-thread hitch)
foreach (var e in em.GetAllEntities()) { names.Add(em.GetName(e)); }

// ✅ GOOD — cached query, Burst job, no managed alloc in the hot path
Entities.WithAll<ShipTag, Simulate>().ForEach(/* Burst-safe math */).ScheduleParallel();
```
